var e=[{id:`fundamental-back-of-envelope`,pathId:`system-design-foundations`,order:0,title:`Back of the Envelope`,subtitle:`Back-of-envelope estimation is the skill that turns "design Twitter" into "your design needs 6k QPS of writes and 600k of reads." Two minutes of math anchors every subsequent decis`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The postmortem took 40 minutes. The math takes two.`,paragraphs:[`Launch night for the streak feature. Three million daily users, a marketing push, confetti in the release channel. When someone asked how the database would hold up, the answer was vibes: 'a few hundred writes a second — Postgres will laugh at that.' Nobody multiplied anything. Not once, not on any whiteboard, not in any doc.`,`At 03:12 the primary pegged at 100% CPU. Replication lag hit 40 minutes, replicas started serving stale reads, support tickets lit up, and clients began retrying — which doubled the write load on a system that was already dying. Here's the number nobody computed: 3M users x 8 events/day = 24M writes/day = ~280 writes/sec average. Fine. But 4x diurnal peak x 5x launch spike x 2x retry storm = ~11k writes/sec, and a synchronous-commit primary with zero batching tops out well below that. We hadn't sized the system; we'd parked it on a cliff edge with the handbrake on.`,`The postmortem took 40 minutes of whiteboard to derive what two minutes of envelope math would have handed us before launch day. The math was always cheap. We just bought it at 3 AM prices.`],problem:`Nobody converted users x actions x peak factors into a write-QPS budget before choosing a single synchronous-commit Postgres primary — the architecture was picked before the math ever ran.`},tech:{stack:[`Back-of-Envelope`,`Powers of Ten`,`QPS Budgets`,`Peak Factors`,`Postgres`,`Redis Cache`,`CDN`,`Write Fanout`],note:`The envelope arithmetic plus the infrastructure it buys or vetoes: per-machine budgets decide whether one Postgres primary survives, or whether sharding, a cache tier, and a CDN get invented on the whiteboard.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why '100M users' tells you nothing`,body:[`Here's the thing nobody tells you: product requirements arrive in units of people, and engineering decisions live in units of per-second. '100M users' is a vibe. '6k writes/sec' is a decision — it tells you whether one Postgres primary carries it or you're sharding on day one. The entire skill is converting people into per-seconds before anyone falls in love with a box diagram.`,`Why not skip the math and just load test? Because a load test needs a target. You'd be generating traffic against a number you made up anyway — so make it up on paper first, where it costs nothing and takes ninety seconds. The envelope is the cheapest experiment you will ever run.`,`And precision is a trap. Nobody cares if it's 5.2k or 6.1k QPS — the architecture that fits 5k fits 6k. What kills designs is being off by an order of magnitude, and that's exactly what two minutes of arithmetic prevents.`],quote:`The number is the design. Without it you're just naming boxes.`,table:{headers:[`Product says`,`Envelope conversion`,`Engineering decision`],rows:[[`10M DAU, 2 posts/day`,`10^7 x 2 / 10^5 sec`,`~200 writes/sec — one primary, done`],[`10M DAU, 50 reads/day`,`10^7 x 50 / 10^5 sec`,`~5k reads/sec — cache starts earning its keep`],[`10M DAU, 3 photos/day @ 2 MB`,`10^7 x 3 x 2 MB`,`~60 TB/day — object store + CDN, never the DB`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Back of the Envelope`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-back-of-envelope.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the ladder you memorize once`,body:[`You don't memorize everything — you memorize a ladder and convert everything else. Latency: RAM at ~100 nanoseconds, SSD read at ~100 microseconds, spinning-disk seek at ~5 milliseconds, cross-region round trip at ~100 milliseconds. Every cache, every replica, every CDN is a bet against that ladder. Four numbers, and suddenly 'should we cache this?' is arithmetic instead of opinion.`,`Time converts with one trick: a day is about 10^5 seconds (86,400, but who's counting), and a year is about 3x10^7 — remember it as pi x 10^7, a coincidence that actually holds. So DAU divided by 10^5 is your average per-second rate, directly. 500M users doing one thing a day? 5k per second. Done, next question.`,`Throughput per machine is the last rung: a web server serves ~10k QPS, Redis does ~100k ops/sec, a SQL primary absorbs ~5k-20k writes/sec with batching, a Kafka broker pushes ~100 MB/sec. These are ballparks, not specs — but they're the right ballparks, and they're what your envelope gets compared against.`],quote:`Memorize the ladder once; convert everything else forever.`,table:{headers:[`Thing`,`Order of magnitude`,`What it decides`],rows:[[`RAM read`,`~100 ns`,`in-process cache is effectively free`],[`SSD read`,`~100 µs`,`DB can serve, but not at scale`],[`HDD seek`,`~5 ms`,`spinning rust is for archives`],[`Cross-region RTT`,`~100 ms`,`sync replication across regions hurts`],[`SQL primary writes`,`~5k-20k /sec`,`shard or batch above this`],[`Redis ops`,`~100k /sec`,`cache layer absorbs the read crowd`],[`Web server QPS`,`~10k /sec`,`horizontal scale is routine`],[`Kafka broker`,`~100 MB/sec`,`one broker per ~10 TB/day ingest`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Back of the Envelope`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-back-of-envelope.html`}},{heading:`03 · A worked example: Twitter in ninety seconds`,body:[`Let's do it live. 500M DAU. Each user writes one tweet a day and reads a hundred. Writes: 500M divided by 10^5 seconds is ~5k/sec average — call it 6k with peak. Reads: 500M x 100 divided by 10^5 is ~500k/sec. Ninety seconds, and we haven't drawn a single box.`,`Now the numbers start making decisions for us. 500k reads/sec against a database that happily serves maybe 20k means we need a 96%+ cache hit rate — the cache isn't an optimization, it's load-bearing. Writes at 5k/sec fit one primary today but not with headroom, so storage is sharded from the start. And media never touches the primary — CDN, full stop.`,`Then the fanout wrinkle. An average user has a few hundred followers, so a tweet is a few hundred timeline writes — fine. A celebrity has ten million. One post from them is ten million writes, and push-fanout dies right there. That's why the hybrid model exists: push timelines for mortals, pull-on-load for celebrities. We didn't invent that from cleverness — the envelope handed it to us.`],quote:`Numbers don't just size the system — they pick the architecture.`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`Envelope math`,`Design consequence`],rows:[[`Write QPS`,`500M / 10^5 = ~5k/sec`,`sharded storage, batched writes`],[`Read QPS`,`500M x 100 / 10^5 = ~500k/sec`,`cache with 96%+ hit rate; DB never sees the crowd`],[`Celebrity fanout`,`1 post x 10M followers`,`hybrid push/pull timelines`],[`Media`,`photos + video per day`,`CDN in front, object storage behind`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Back of the Envelope`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-back-of-envelope.html`}},{heading:`04 · Peak factors, retries, and the lies averages tell`,body:[`Here's where our 3 AM story actually went wrong. The average was fine — 280 writes/sec, Postgres yawns. But traffic isn't average. Diurnal peak runs 2-4x the mean. A launch or a viral moment adds 5-10x on top. And when you start timing out, clients retry, which is another 2x — the cruelest multiplier, because it only shows up when you're already dying. Stack them and 280 becomes 11k.`,`So the rule: budget for roughly 10x your average, and keep the machine under half its rated capacity at that peak. Why not just autoscale? Because the write path is stateful — primaries don't grow in thirty seconds, and the retry storm arrives in thirty. The envelope sets the floor; autoscaling handles the weather above it.`,`And one number with no upper bound: fanout. One user with ten million followers turns one write into ten million. When any single event can be unbounded, you don't size for it — you queue it, shed it, or isolate it. The envelope tells you which events are bombs; the architecture is where you defuse them.`],quote:`Average is a marketing number. Peak is an engineering number.`,table:{headers:[`Multiplier`,`Typical factor`,`Design implication`],rows:[[`Diurnal peak`,`2-4x average`,`size for the evening, not the mean`],[`Launch / viral spike`,`5-10x`,`pre-provision or queue at the edge`],[`Retry storm`,`~2x`,`timeouts + backoff, or it doubles your worst day`],[`Celebrity fanout`,`unbounded`,`queue, shed, or isolate — never inline`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Back of the Envelope`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-back-of-envelope.html`}},{heading:`05 · Where the envelope is wrong on purpose`,body:[`Let's be honest about what this is: deliberately sloppy arithmetic. You're rounding everything to one significant digit, and that's the point. If your estimate is off by 2x, the architecture survives — same boxes, slightly bigger. Off by 10x, it doesn't — different boxes entirely. The entire game is getting the exponent right, and exponents survive aggressive rounding.`,`The discipline is the sanity check at the end: does the answer fit on one machine, one rack, or one region? 'We need 60 TB of storage a day' instantly disqualifies a dozen cute ideas. 'We need 200 writes/sec' disqualifies the entire microservice carnival — one Postgres and a good night's sleep. The envelope is a filter, and its job is killing options cheaply.`,`And in the room — interview or incident review — the signal isn't a perfect number, it's that you produce one at all. 'About 5k QPS' sounds senior. 'I have no idea' sounds like the 3 AM page. Two minutes of math is the cheapest seniority you can buy.`],quote:`Get the exponent right and stop there — decimals are for capacity planning, not architecture.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Back of the Envelope`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-back-of-envelope.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`envelope-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`From product spec to architecture in five arrows`,caption:`The conversion chain: people in, per-seconds out, and every branch point is a decision the number makes for you.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    A["Product spec:<br/>500M DAU"] --> B["Actions per user:<br/>1 write, 100 reads per day"]
    B --> C["Divide by 10^5 sec/day"]
    C --> D["~5k writes/sec avg"]
    C --> E["~500k reads/sec avg"]
    D --> F{"One primary handles<br/>~5k-20k writes/sec?"}
    E --> G{"DB serves ~20k reads/sec?"}
    F -- "borderline: need headroom" --> H["Sharded storage<br/>+ batched writes"]
    G -- "no: need 96%+ hit rate" --> I["Cache tier<br/>+ CDN for media"]
    H --> J["Architecture locked<br/>in 90 seconds"]
    I --> J`},{id:`launch-incident`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The 3 AM retry storm, annotated with the math we skipped`,caption:`280 writes/sec became 11k the moment peak factors and client retries stacked — and every hop here was predictable on a whiteboard.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as Users
    participant A as API tier
    participant P as Postgres primary
    participant R as Read replicas

    U->>A: Launch peak ~5.6k writes/sec (280 avg x 4x diurnal x 5x launch)
    A->>P: INSERT per event, fsync per commit, no batching
    P-->>A: p99 climbs past 500 ms
    Note over A: clients time out and retry
    U->>A: retry storm doubles load to ~11k writes/sec
    A->>P: queue depth explodes
    P->>R: WAL shipping falls behind
    Note over P,R: replication lag hits 40 minutes
    R-->>A: stale reads served
    Note over P: 03:12 — primary CPU pegged, page fires`}],takeaways:[`Memorize the latency ladder once: RAM ~100 ns, SSD ~100 µs, disk seek ~5 ms, cross-region ~100 ms — every cache and replica is a bet against these four numbers.`,`A day is ~10^5 seconds and a year ~3x10^7 (pi x 10^7): so DAU / 10^5 x actions-per-day = average QPS in one step.`,`Design for ~10x average, never the average: 2-4x diurnal peak x 5-10x launch spike x 2x retry storm turned our 280 writes/sec into 11k.`,`Know the per-machine budgets: web server ~10k QPS, Redis ~100k ops/sec, SQL primary ~5k-20k writes/sec, Kafka broker ~100 MB/sec (~10 TB/day) — compare your envelope against these before drawing boxes.`,`Off by 2x, the architecture survives; off by 10x, it doesn't — get the exponent right, sanity-check 'one machine, one rack, or one region?', and remember 'about 5k QPS' always beats 'I have no idea.'`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Back of the Envelope`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-back-of-envelope.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — Networking Essentials for System Design Interviews w/ Ex Meta Senior Manager`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SHkbPm1Wrno`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Networking essentials you need before estimating.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-api-design`,pathId:`system-design-foundations`,order:1,title:`API Design Best Practices`,subtitle:`Public APIs live for a decade. The shape you ship is the shape you maintain. Get the conventions right at the start — resource naming, errors, pagination, idempotency, versioning —`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`JSON`,`OpenAPI`,`REST`,`idempotency`],story:{headline:`The double-charge that cost us a year`,paragraphs:[`11:47 PM, the night before Black Friday. Our biggest customer's checkout hits a gateway timeout mid-payment and does what any sane client does: it retries. We charge the card twice. Worse — their reconciliation job, built on our offset pagination, has been silently double-counting orders for a week, and their finance team finds out from an end-customer complaint, not from us.`,`The postmortem found no bug. It found conventions we skipped in month one to 'move fast.' Errors were a bare string — 'something went wrong' — so their SDK couldn't tell a timeout from a decline. No idempotency keys, so every retry was a fresh charge. No versioning, so every fix we shipped broke someone. And POST /createCharge in the URL, because verbs-in-URLs felt 'more obvious' at the time.`,`We spent the next twelve months building v2: plural resources, structured errors with request IDs, cursor pagination, idempotent writes, an OpenAPI spec, signed webhooks. Every convention in this lesson has an invoice attached to it. Here's what we learned — so you can pay the price of a webinar instead of the price of a year.`],problem:`An API's shape is a decade-long commitment: conventions skipped at launch come back as double charges, migration guides, and a year-long rewrite.`},tech:{stack:[`REST`,`JSON`,`OpenAPI`,`Cursor Pagination`,`Idempotency Keys`,`Structured Errors`,`HMAC Webhooks`,`Semantic Versioning`],note:`The contract surface a public API lives on — naming, errors, pagination, retries, and evolution. These pieces decide whether year three is a feature release or a rewrite.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why /createCharge haunts you`,body:[`Let's start with the thing everyone gets wrong in week one. Resources are plural nouns — /users, /orders, /charges. The verb already lives in the HTTP method: POST /charges creates a charge, GET /charges/{id} reads one, DELETE deletes. When I see POST /createCharge, I know the designer never let the protocol do its job — they're rebuilding RPC on top of HTTP and losing caching, semantics, and tooling along the way.`,`Relationships are sub-resources: GET /users/{id}/orders. But here's the trap — keep it two levels deep, max. /users/{id}/orders/{id}/items/{id}/refunds feels precise today and becomes an unmaintainable cave by v2. And for actions that aren't CRUD? Don't invent a magic PATCH body field. Use a verb sub-resource — POST /orders/{id}/cancel, POST /charges/{id}/refund. Explicit, auditable, versionable.`],quote:`Your URL is a ten-year promise — /createCharge is a promise you'll spend year three apologizing for.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`REST resources`,`Cacheable, standard tooling, predictable`,`Requires naming discipline`,`Public APIs`],[`RPC-style URLs`,`Familiar to app devs, explicit actions`,`No caching, broken semantics, drifts`,`Internal-only, narrow`],[`Verb sub-resources`,`Explicit non-CRUD actions`,`Slight extra design cost`,`Refund, cancel, retry ops`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Design Best Practices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-design.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: status codes and error bodies`,body:[`Next, the error contract — because this is where integrations go to die. Everything-returns-200-with-{ok:false} is the single worst habit in API design. Your SDK, your retry logic, your monitoring — all of them key off status codes. Break that, and every client builds bespoke parsing against your 'convention.'`,`The body matters as much as the code. Three fields: code — the stable machine identifier clients switch on; message — human-readable, free to change any time; request_id — the string support pastes into a trace search. And learn the distinctions: 400 (malformed JSON) vs 422 (valid JSON, invalid semantics), 401 (who are you?) vs 403 (I know who you are — no), 409 for state conflicts like an idempotency-key mismatch.`],quote:`A 200 with {ok:false} is a lie your SDK will repeat forever.`,table:{headers:[`Code`,`Use`],rows:[[`200`,`OK with body (read or update)`],[`201 Created`,`POST created a resource — include Location header`],[`204 No Content`,`Success, no body`],[`400`,`Malformed input`],[`401 / 403`,`No valid auth / authed but not allowed`],[`404`,`Resource doesn't exist`],[`409`,`State conflict — duplicate key, idempotency mismatch`],[`422`,`Valid JSON, invalid semantics`],[`429`,`Rate-limited — include Retry-After`],[`500 / 503`,`Server bug / overload — 503 carries Retry-After`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Design Best Practices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-design.html`}},{heading:`03 · Pagination: why offset lies to you`,body:[`Here's the one that bit us on Black Friday. Offset pagination — ?offset=40&limit=20 — assumes the dataset holds still. It never does. A new order lands at position zero, every offset shifts, and your customer's reconciliation job sees duplicates on the next page and silently skips rows. Nobody notices until finance does.`,`Cursor pagination fixes this: ?limit=20&cursor=eyJpZCI6Li4ufQ. The cursor is an opaque, base64-encoded 'where we left off' — typically the last ID. Inserts ahead of you don't move it. Return next_cursor and has_more on every page, and if you're feeling generous, a hypertext next link — most clients won't use it, but it costs you one line of JSON.`],quote:`Offsets count positions in a moving crowd; cursors remember the last person you shook hands with.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Offset`,`Simple, jump-to-page`,`Duplicates/skips on insert, slow deep pages`,`Static datasets, admin UIs`],[`Cursor`,`Stable under writes, fast at depth`,`No random access, opaque to clients`,`Feeds, lists, public APIs`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Design Best Practices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-design.html`}},{heading:`04 · Idempotency: writes that survive the network`,body:[`Now the 3 AM lesson. Networks time out. Clients retry. That's not a bug — that's physics. If POST /charges isn't idempotent, every timeout is a potential double charge. The fix is the Idempotency-Key header: the client generates a UUID per logical operation, the server stores key → response, and a retry replays the stored answer instead of re-executing.`,`Two rules make it airtight. One: if the same key arrives with a different payload, that's a client bug — return 409 Conflict, don't guess. Two: keep the stored response long enough to outlive realistic retries — 24 hours minimum. Pair this with 429 and 503 responses that carry Retry-After, so backoff becomes a protocol instead of folklore.`],quote:`The network retries your writes whether you designed for it or not — the only question is whether you get the same answer twice.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Server behavior`,`Status`],rows:[[`First request, new key`,`Execute and store response`,`201`],[`Retry, same key + payload`,`Replay stored response`,`201`],[`Same key, different payload`,`Reject — client bug`,`409`],[`Key expired`,`Execute as a new operation`,`201`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Design Best Practices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-design.html`}},{heading:`05 · Evolution: versioning, specs, and the headers clients actually read`,body:[`Last block — the decade view. The shape you ship is the shape you maintain, so bake evolution in from day one. Version in the URL (/v2/charges) or a header — pick one, document it, and never break within a version. Ship the OpenAPI spec as build output, not a wiki page; the spec is the contract, and SDKs and codegen fall out of it for free.`,`And the small stuff that separates pros from hobbyists: timestamps in ISO 8601, identifiers as UUIDs, rate limits in response headers (X-RateLimit-Remaining, Retry-After) where clients can read them programmatically, and webhooks signed with HMAC plus a timestamp to kill replays. None of it is glamorous. All of it is why your fifth integrator ships in a day instead of a quarter.`],quote:`A spec is a contract; a wiki page is a rumor.`,table:{headers:[`Versioning`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`URL path (/v2/)`,`Visible, cache-friendly, easy routing`,`URL churn on major bumps`,`Most public APIs`],[`Header (Accept)`,`Clean URLs`,`Invisible, harder to debug`,`Hypermedia-leaning APIs`],[`No versioning`,`Zero upfront cost`,`Every fix is breaking`,`Nothing. Ever.`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Design Best Practices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-design.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Every request walks the same gauntlet`,caption:`Auth, rate limit, validate, then the idempotency fork — and every failure exits through the same structured error body.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Client request"] --> B["Auth middleware"]
    B -- "401 invalid auth" --> ERR["Structured error: code + message + request_id"]
    B --> C["Rate limiter"]
    C -- "429 + Retry-After" --> ERR
    C --> D["Validate payload"]
    D -- "400 malformed / 422 semantic" --> ERR
    D --> E{"Write request?"}
    E -- "GET read" --> H["Handler"]
    E -- "POST + Idempotency-Key" --> F{"Key seen before?"}
    F -- "same payload" --> G["Replay stored response"]
    F -- "different payload" --> I["409 Conflict"]
    F -- "new key" --> H
    H --> J["201 Created + Location header"]
    H -- "500 server bug" --> ERR
    G --> J
    I --> ERR`},{id:`idempotent-retry`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One charge, two requests, zero double-billing`,caption:`The timeout-and-retry sequence that would have saved our Black Friday.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as API
    participant K as Idempotency Store
    participant P as Payment Processor
    C->>A: POST /charges (Idempotency-Key: k1)
    A->>K: lookup k1
    K-->>A: miss
    A->>P: create charge $49.99
    P-->>A: charge_812 confirmed
    A->>K: store k1 with 201 response
    A-->>C: 201 Created
    Note over C,A: response lost to a gateway timeout
    C->>A: retry POST /charges (Idempotency-Key: k1)
    A->>K: lookup k1
    K-->>A: hit — stored 201 for charge_812
    A-->>C: 201 Created (replayed)
    Note over C,P: two requests, one charge, zero double-billing`}],takeaways:[`Cap resource paths at two levels deep (/users/{id}/orders); if you need a third level, you need a new resource.`,`Every error response carries exactly three fields — stable code, human message, request_id — and clients switch on code, never on message text.`,`Cursor pagination with limit ≤ 100; offset pagination corrupts pages the instant a row inserts ahead of the reader.`,`Require Idempotency-Key on every POST; store key→response for ≥ 24 hours; replay on retry, 409 on payload mismatch.`,`Ship the OpenAPI spec as build output, version in the URL (/v2/), and put rate limits plus Retry-After in headers — not in docs.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — API Design Best Practices`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-design.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — API Design in System Design Interviews w/ Meta Staff Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DQ57zYedMdQ`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`API design under interview pressure.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-api-versioning`,pathId:`system-design-foundations`,order:2,title:`API Versioning`,subtitle:`"How do we evolve an API without breaking clients" is the question. Four strategies, each with passionate defenders. The honest answer: it depends on who your callers are and how m`,minutes:8,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`URL`,`date-based`,`header`,`sunset`],story:{headline:`The bug fix that broke 14,000 invoices at 3 AM`,paragraphs:["It was 3:07 AM when the pager went off: a partner's nightly reconciliation was failing and 14,000 invoices were stuck in `failed`. Six hours earlier we had shipped what the release notes politely called a validation cleanup. Our `amount` field was documented as integer cents, but for three years the server had silently truncated floats — someone sent `10.50`, we charged `10`. A new engineer spotted it, wrote the fix, started returning a 400 for non-integer amounts. Textbook bug fix. Except one partner's billing job had been sending floats the entire time, and it had been working.",`The partner's ticket read like an archaeology report: the integration had been built by a vendor they acquired in 2019, that vendor's engineers were long gone, and the code was 'not modifiable without a contract renewal.' There was no human to email behind the traffic — just a cron job in a basement somewhere, faithfully sending floats. We rolled the validation back by 4 AM, and I spent the rest of the quarter building the thing I should have built on day one: a version boundary.`,`The lesson that stuck: your API contract is not what you documented — it's what every client, including the ones you can't name, has compiled against. Tightening enforcement is a breaking change, full stop. Versioning is how you buy the right to fix things without holding 14,000 invoices hostage at 3 AM.`],problem:`How do you evolve a published contract that thousands of clients depend on — including integrations you can't email, can't invoice, and can't afford to break?`},tech:{stack:[`URL Path /v1`,`Versioned Host`,`Media-Type Header`,`Stripe-Style Dates`,`Sunset Header`,`Compat Shims`],note:`These are the four places a version can live — path, host, Accept media-type, date header — plus the machinery that keeps old versions survivable: Sunset headers and per-version compat shims over one shared core.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just don't break it' fails`,body:[`Here's the uncomfortable math. The moment you publish an API, every client compiles against it — and you lose control of the deploy schedule for all of them. If it's internal, you control both ends and you can just... coordinate. Slack message, migration PR, done. The moment it's public, your caller list includes apps in someone's basement, companies that got acquired, vendors that went under. Some of them will literally never update.`,`So the real question isn't 'how do we avoid breaking changes' — you can't; the product evolves. It's 'how do we ship a breaking change while the un-upgradable clients keep working.' That's all versioning is: a mechanism for running two contracts at once, with a plan for retiring the old one.`,`And notice what the 3 AM story taught us: it's not the changes you document that bite you. It's the accidents you've been tolerating. Clients don't read your docs — they read your behavior.`],quote:`The API contract is the public face of the company. Breaking it without a path forward burns trust no marketing can rebuild.`,table:{headers:[`Caller profile`,`Can you reach them?`,`What that implies`],rows:[[`First-party frontend`,`Yes — same repo or team`,`Skip versioning; coordinate deploys`],[`Internal teams`,`Mostly — pingable`,`Lightweight: deprecation notices, 90-day windows`],[`Known integrators`,`Partly — emails, dashboards`,`URL path majors + Sunset headers`],[`Long-tail public`,`No — basement apps, dead vendors`,`Date-based pinning; never force-migrate`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Versioning`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-versioning.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where does the version live?`,body:[`Four places to put the version, and each one is really a bet about who's reading it. URL path — /v1/charges — is the crowd favorite because it's visible everywhere: logs, curl, browser, load balancer rules. The purists object that the URI identifies a resource and the resource didn't change, and they're technically right and practically ignored — GitHub, Twilio, and Stripe all use path versioning for major versions.`,`Host versioning — v1.api.example.com — is the same idea with a bigger hammer: you can route an entire version to different infrastructure, which matters if v2 runs on a different stack. In practice it's rare, because it's path versioning with extra DNS and certificate setup and no additional client clarity.`,`Then the two header schemes. Media-type versioning — Accept: application/vnd.example.v1+json — keeps one URL and lets the client ask for the representation. Beautifully RESTful, painful to curl by hand, fine inside an SDK; GitHub's preview headers are the famous example. And Stripe's date-based scheme — Stripe-Version: 2024-11-20 — is the most disciplined of all: every breaking change gets a date, clients pin once and upgrade on their own schedule, and the API publishes a changelog per date. The trade: your server now maintains compatibility for every date you've ever published.`],quote:`You don't pick a versioning strategy — you pick the support burden you're willing to carry for ten years.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`URL path /v1/`,`Obvious in logs, trivial routing, curl-friendly`,`Purist objection; URI churn`,`Default for public APIs`],[`Host v1.api.…`,`Route whole versions to separate infra`,`DNS/TLS overhead; rare in practice`,`Versions on different stacks`],[`Accept media-type`,`Pure REST; one stable URL`,`Hard to debug by hand`,`SDK-first APIs, opt-in previews`],[`Date header (Stripe)`,`Fine-grained; client pins; per-date changelog`,`Server keeps compat for every date`,`Huge, long-tail integrator bases`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Versioning`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-versioning.html`}},{heading:`03 · The tightening trap: what actually counts as breaking`,body:[`Everyone remembers 'don't remove fields.' The one that got us was subtler: tolerant readers — clients that ignore what they don't understand — protect you when you loosen, and abandon you the instant you tighten. If the server starts enforcing something it used to accept, every client that relied on the sloppy behavior just broke, docs notwithstanding. That's exactly how our float-truncation 'bug fix' became an outage.`,`So the rule I run now: any visible-behavior change is breaking. Changing a default, changing the error code for an existing condition, tightening validation — all breaking. If in doubt, version it. A compat shim costs an afternoon; a 3 AM rollback costs a quarter of roadmap.`],quote:`Clients don't read your docs — they read your behavior. Tighten the behavior and you've shipped a breaking change.`,table:{headers:[`Change`,`Breaking?`],rows:[[`Add a new optional field to response`,`No — clients should ignore unknown fields`],[`Add a new endpoint`,`No`],[`Remove a field`,`Yes`],[`Rename a field`,`Yes`],[`Change field type (string → int)`,`Yes`],[`Change a default value`,`Yes — clients may rely on it`],[`Add a required parameter`,`Yes`],[`Change pagination shape`,`Yes`],[`Change error code for an existing condition`,`Yes`],[`Tighten validation`,`Yes — formerly-valid requests now fail`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Versioning`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-versioning.html`}},{heading:`04 · The operational bill: every version has a payroll`,body:[`Here's what nobody puts in the blog post: whichever strategy you pick, you've just signed up to run N contracts simultaneously. Path versioning looks cheap until you're maintaining two full handler sets and a double test matrix. Date-based looks expensive until you realize the compat layer is mostly mechanical shims over one shared core — transform in, transform out.`,`The machinery that makes old versions survivable is boring and non-negotiable: a Sunset header with an actual date on every response from a deprecated version, a public changelog, and a per-version traffic dashboard so you know who's still out there. And when long-tail traffic on v1 drops to almost nothing, you don't just flip it off — you email the holdouts, because at that volume each one is often a whale.`],quote:`Every version you publish is a promise with a payroll — budget the compat layer when you ship the breaking change, not after.`,table:{headers:[`Mechanism`,`What it does`,`Where clients meet it`],rows:[[`Sunset header`,`States the kill date on every response`,`Automated deprecation alerts`],[`Deprecation changelog`,`Per-version diff of what changed`,`Upgrade planning`],[`Per-version traffic dashboard`,`Shows who is still on what`,`Your sunset go/no-go decision`],[`410 Gone + upgrade hint`,`Clean failure after the date`,`Last-resort migration nudge`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Versioning`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-versioning.html`}},{heading:`05 · Picking one: match the strategy to the caller`,body:[`So here's the decision I actually make now, and it starts with the caller list, not with taste. Internal only, both ends yours? Don't version — coordinate. A dozen known integrators? URL path majors, bumped rarely, with Sunset headers and a real deprecation window. Thousands of untrusted, un-emailable callers? That's date-based pinning territory — Stripe's model exists precisely because their long tail is enormous.`,`And note that the pros don't pick just one: Stripe runs path majors and date minors together. /v1 tells you the big contract generation; the date tells you the exact behavior within it. Hybrid isn't a compromise — it's two dials for two different kinds of change.`],quote:`Version for the callers you can't call — everyone else, you can just talk to.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — API Versioning`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-versioning.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`edge-negotiation`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Version negotiation at the edge — four strategies, one core`,caption:`However the client names the version, it resolves to an adapter over shared domain logic. Versions are shims, not forks — that's what keeps the compat bill survivable.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Clients
    C1["Mobile app<br/>pinned to /v1"]
    C2["Partner SDK<br/>Accept vnd.example.v2+json"]
    C3["Legacy cron<br/>no version header"]
  end
  GW["API Gateway"]
  VR{"Version Resolver"}
  A1["v1 Adapter<br/>frozen contract"]
  A2["v2 Adapter<br/>current contract"]
  CL["Date Compat Layer<br/>one shim per release date"]
  CORE["Shared Core<br/>domain logic"]
  DB[("Postgres")]
  C1 --> GW
  C2 --> GW
  C3 --> GW
  GW --> VR
  VR -->|"path /v1"| A1
  VR -->|"path /v2"| A2
  VR -->|"Accept media type"| A2
  VR -->|"Stripe-Version date"| CL
  A1 --> CORE
  A2 --> CORE
  CL --> CORE
  CORE --> DB`},{id:`pinned-date-request`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`A date-pinned request through the compat layer`,caption:`Stripe-style versioning in motion: the client pins a date once; the resolver either serves that exact contract or fails cleanly with an upgrade hint — it never silently changes behavior under a pinned client.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client SDK pinned to 2024-06-01
  participant G as API Gateway
  participant R as Compat Resolver
  participant S as Core Service
  C->>G: POST /v1/charges with Stripe-Version 2024-06-01
  G->>R: resolve pinned date
  alt date exists in changelog
    R->>S: canonical request
    S-->>R: canonical response
    R-->>G: shape response to 2024-06-01 contract
    G-->>C: 200 OK — old shape, old fields
  else date unknown or retired
    R-->>G: reject
    G-->>C: 400 with upgrade hint and changelog link
  end`}],takeaways:[`Public API with a broad integrator base: URL path majors (/v1, /v2), bumped no more than once every 12–24 months — use date-based versioning as the inner dial for finer behavior changes.`,`Give every deprecated version a Sunset header with a real date: 90 days minimum for internal callers, 6–12 months for public integrators — and send it on every response, not just in the announcement post.`,`Treat every tightening change as breaking — remove, rename, retype, re-default, required param, pagination shape, error-code change, tightened validation. If in doubt, version it: a shim costs hours, a rollback costs a quarter.`,`Instrument per-version traffic per API key from day one; when a version falls below ~1% of calls for 90 days, email the holdouts before sunsetting — at that point each holdout is usually a whale, not noise.`,`Pick by caller count, not aesthetics: 1–10 internal callers → no versioning, just coordinate; 10–1,000 known integrators → path majors + Sunset headers; 1,000+ un-emailable callers → Stripe-style date pinning.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — API Versioning`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-api-versioning.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-load-balancing`,pathId:`system-design-foundations`,order:3,title:`Load Balancing Algorithms`,subtitle:`Round-robin works for stateless homogeneous backends. Least-connections beats it when request durations vary. Power-of-two-choices beats both when you can afford the lookup. Consis`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Envoy`,`NGINX`,`YARP`,`gRPC client LB`],story:{headline:`One pod on fire, three on vacation`,paragraphs:[`2:47 AM. PagerDuty: payments p99 latency tripled. I open the dash half-asleep and everything looks... fine. Load balancer: 4/4 targets healthy, connections flowing. Then I scroll to the pod graphs: pod-1 at 98% CPU and getting throttled, pods 2 through 4 idling at 7%. Our 'load balanced' service was a monoculture — one machine doing all the work, three chaperones standing around it.`,`The culprit was a decision made six months earlier with zero drama: put the new gRPC service behind the NLB, same as everything else. What nobody put on the slide: gRPC is HTTP/2. Every client holds ONE long-lived TCP connection and multiplexes thousands of requests through it. The NLB balances connections — it pinned each client's single connection to a single pod and walked away. Our heaviest client, the nightly settlement batch, pushed ten thousand requests a minute through its one pinned connection into pod-1. Connection-level round-robin had produced a request-level monopoly.`,`The fix took twenty minutes; the bruise lasted longer. Envoy in front, per-request balancing with P2C — and then the second lesson of the night: least-connections for the report-generation endpoints, because a 2ms lookup and a 900ms export are not the same unit of work. The balancer was never broken. It was answering a question we never thought to ask: balanced by what?`],problem:`Why did a healthy load balancer showing 4/4 targets send one client's entire firehose to a single pod?`},tech:{stack:[`NGINX`,`Envoy`,`AWS NLB`,`HAProxy`,`gRPC`,`HTTP/2`,`P2C`,`Consistent Hashing`],note:`The balancers, wire protocols, and algorithms this story collides with — from the L4 box that balanced the wrong unit, to the L7 proxy and the algorithm that finally matched the workload.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: balanced by WHAT, exactly?`,body:[`Here's what I kept saying at 3 AM: 'but we're load balanced!' And we were — by one definition. 'Spread requests evenly across N backends' sounds like one sentence, but 'evenly' is doing all the work. Evenly by request count? By CPU consumed? By concurrent in-flight connections? By recent response time? Each definition is a different algorithm, and each wrong assumption is a different 3 AM page.`,`Round-robin is even by request count. Least-connections is even by in-flight count. Least-latency is even by recent response time. If your requests cost wildly different amounts — a 2ms cache hit sitting next to a 900ms export — then count-fairness is unfairness with extra steps. One backend serving ten thousand quick reads is doing less work than one serving ten thousand exports.`,`So the first move isn't picking an algorithm. It's picking your unit of fairness. Everything else follows from that.`],quote:`Round-robin only looks fair because we agreed not to measure what we actually care about.`,table:{headers:[`Fairness metric`,`Algorithm`,`Breaks when`],rows:[[`Request count`,`Round-robin`,`Request costs vary`],[`In-flight connections`,`Least-connections`,`LB can't track per-backend state`],[`Recent latency`,`Least-latency / P2C variants`,`Latency signal is noisy`],[`Key locality`,`Consistent hashing`,`Hot keys skew one shard`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Load Balancing Algorithms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-load-balancing.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the shelf: four algorithms, four backend shapes`,body:[`Let's walk the shelf. Round-robin: cycle 1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3. Stateless on the balancer, trivial to implement, totally predictable — and exactly right for one backend shape: stateless, homogeneous, uniform-cost. A fleet of static file servers? Ship it. Anything with variable request cost? You're now distributing work unevenly with great confidence.`,`Least-connections picks the backend with the fewest active connections, and it self-corrects: a slow backend accumulates in-flight work, stops receiving new traffic, drains, recovers. That's why it's the default in HAProxy and NGINX's least_conn. Then P2C — my favorite 'wait, that's it?' algorithm: pick two backends at random, send to the lighter one. Sounds like a coin flip with extra steps; performs near-optimally at scale, and it sidesteps the herd effect where a dozen distributed proxies all 'independently' pick the same least-loaded pod. That's why Linkerd ships it by default.`,`And consistent hashing isn't really a fairness algorithm at all — it's a locality algorithm. Hash a key (user ID, session, partition) and the same key lands on the same backend, modulo membership changes. You reach for it when the backend holds state: session affinity, in-process caches. You're trading fairness for stickiness.`],quote:`P2C sounds like a joke and performs like a theorem — two random darts get you within a hair of optimal.`,table:{headers:[`Algorithm`,`Shines on`,`Fails on`,`Where you'll meet it`],rows:[[`Round-robin`,`Stateless, uniform-cost fleets`,`Variable request durations`,`NGINX default, DNS RR`],[`Least-connections`,`Mixed request durations`,`Needs per-backend state; herd effect when distributed`,`HAProxy default, NGINX least_conn`],[`P2C`,`Large fleets, distributed proxies`,`Tiny fleets — randomness too coarse`,`Linkerd data plane`],[`Consistent hashing`,`Stateful backends, caches`,`Hot keys; fairness-blind`,`Session affinity, cache rings`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Load Balancing Algorithms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-load-balancing.html`}},{heading:`03 · The L4 trap that lit the fire`,body:[`Now the incident itself, because it deserves an autopsy. Load balancers live at two layers. L4 — AWS NLB, Azure Load Balancer, Cloudflare Spectrum — balances TCP connections at line rate. Fast, simple, and completely blind to what's inside the pipe. L7 — ALB, NGINX, Envoy, YARP — terminates HTTP, parses it, and can route per request by URL, header, or cookie.`,`Here's the trap: HTTP/2 and gRPC multiplex many requests over ONE TCP connection. An L4 balancer sees one connection, pins it to one backend, done. Every request your client makes for the next hour rides that single pinned connection into one pod. Connection-level round-robin produced a request-level monopoly — and our dashboards said 'balanced' because we were graphing the wrong unit.`,`So the rule I tattooed on the team wiki: gRPC behind an L4 balancer is a misconfiguration, not a choice. You need L7 per-request balancing — Envoy, Linkerd — or client-side load balancing where the gRPC channel itself spreads requests across resolved endpoints.`],quote:`An L4 balancer balances connections. gRPC hides ten thousand requests inside one connection. Do the math.`,table:{headers:[``,`L4 (NLB, Azure LB)`,`L7 (ALB, Envoy, NGINX)`],rows:[[`Unit of balance`,`TCP connection`,`HTTP request`],[`Speed`,`Line rate, kernel-fast`,`Per-request proxy overhead`],[`Sees`,`IPs and ports, nothing else`,`URLs, headers, cookies`],[`gRPC fit`,`One conn → one pod`,`Per-request balancing`],[`Use when`,`Raw TCP, databases, non-HTTP`,`HTTP APIs, gRPC, routing rules`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Load Balancing Algorithms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-load-balancing.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why not least-connections everywhere?`,body:[`After the incident, the temptation is to swing hard the other way: least-connections for everything, done. But think it through. Least-connections needs a per-backend counter, and in a distributed setup — every proxy with its own view — you get the herd effect: all the proxies look at the same fleet, all see the same 'lightest' backend, all pick it in the same millisecond. You've built a synchronized stampede.`,`P2C is the escape hatch: randomness decorrelates the decisions. Two darts, take the lighter, and no two proxies are sampling the same pair at the same instant. That's the deep trick — it's not that P2C is smart, it's that it's independently dumb in a way that averages out to smart.`,`The same 'why not X' logic kills consistent hashing as a fairness tool. It will absolutely not balance evenly — one hot user, one viral tenant, one partition key with ten times the traffic, and one shard is on fire while the ring sits pretty. Hashing optimizes locality. If you need fairness AND locality, you're in bounded-load hashing territory, and that's a different webinar.`],quote:`Fairness and locality are different goals. Consistent hashing isn't trying to be fair — it's trying to be sticky.`,table:{headers:[`Tempting default`,`Why it bites`,`Reach for instead`],rows:[[`Round-robin everywhere`,`Blind to request cost`,`Least-connections or P2C`],[`Strict least-connections, distributed`,`Herd effect on shared view`,`P2C`],[`Consistent hashing for fairness`,`Optimizes locality, not load`,`P2C / least-connections`],[`L4 for gRPC`,`Connection-level granularity`,`L7 proxy or client-side LB`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Load Balancing Algorithms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-load-balancing.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`incident-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The 3 AM topology: L4 balancing connections, not requests`,caption:`Each gRPC client holds one long-lived HTTP/2 connection. The NLB pins each connection to one pod — so the settlement batch's 10k req/min all land on pod-1 while its siblings idle.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    NLB["AWS NLB — L4<br/>balances CONNECTIONS only"]
    C1["settlement batch client<br/>1 long-lived HTTP/2 conn"] --> NLB
    C2["interactive API clients<br/>long-lived HTTP/2 conns"] --> NLB
    NLB ==>|"pinned: ~10k req/min"| P1["pod-1<br/>98% CPU — throttled"]
    NLB -.->|"pinned: trickle"| P2["pod-2<br/>7% CPU"]
    NLB -.-> P3["pod-3<br/>7% CPU"]
    NLB -.-> P4["pod-4<br/>7% CPU"]`},{id:`fix-p2c-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The fix: L7 per-request balancing with P2C`,caption:`Envoy terminates HTTP/2 and re-balances every request independently — two random samples, route to the lighter pod. One connection in, many destinations out.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as gRPC client
    participant E as Envoy — L7
    participant P1 as pod-1 (3 in-flight)
    participant P2 as pod-2 (1 in-flight)
    participant P3 as pod-3 (7 in-flight)
    C->>E: req #1 (shared HTTP/2 conn)
    E->>E: P2C — sample two pods
    Note over E: sampled pod-2 vs pod-3<br/>pod-2 is lighter
    E->>P2: req #1
    P2-->>E: 200 (12 ms)
    E-->>C: 200
    C->>E: req #2 (same conn)
    E->>E: P2C — sample again
    Note over E: sampled pod-1 vs pod-2<br/>pod-1 is lighter now
    E->>P1: req #2
    P1-->>E: 200 (9 ms)
    E-->>C: 200
    Note over C,P3: per-REQUEST balancing —<br/>one connection, many destinations`}],takeaways:[`If p99 request duration is more than ~5x p50, round-robin is already unfair — switch to least-connections or P2C before you tune anything else.`,`gRPC or HTTP/2 behind an L4 balancer pins every client to ONE pod. Put Envoy/Linkerd (L7) or client-side LB in front — this is a correctness fix, not an optimization.`,`At ~20+ backends, P2C lands within a few percent of least-connections while tracking zero global state: two random samples, route to the lighter.`,`Reach for consistent hashing only when backends hold state (sessions, in-proc caches) — and expect hot keys to skew a shard; keep bounded-load hashing or key salting as the escape hatch.`,`Wire active health checks (~1-2s interval) plus passive outlier ejection (e.g., 5 consecutive 5xx → eject for 30s) under whatever algorithm you pick — the algorithm can only choose among backends it knows are alive.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Load Balancing Algorithms`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-load-balancing.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`}];export{e as lessons};